<p>Recently, I learned that macOS allows a regular user process to bind
to a privileged port.</p>
<p>To test this out, in one terminal, do:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code>$<span class="w"> </span>nc<span class="w"> </span>-l<span class="w"> </span><span class="m">0</span>.0.0.0<span class="w"> </span><span class="m">80</span>
</code></pre></div>

<p>In a second terminal, talk to the process listening on port <code>80</code>:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nb">echo</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">&quot;hello world&quot;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">|</span><span class="w"> </span>nc<span class="w"> </span><span class="m">127</span>.0.0.1<span class="w"> </span><span class="m">80</span>
</code></pre></div>

<p>The first terminal should now print:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code>hello world
</code></pre></div>

<p>Cool eh? Happy networking!</p>